  "articleBody": "Natural stone tile can be absolutely stunning, but there are a few common pitfalls to watch out for if you want a perfect finish. First off, a frequent mistake is not ordering enough material. You really need about 10–15% extra tile for cuts, potential breakage, and those 'just in case' future repairs. Another big one is not blending tiles from different boxes. Natural stone has unique variations in color and veining, so mixing them up before installation creates a more harmonious and natural look. Dry cutting your stone indoors is another no-no; it creates silica dust, which isn't good for anyone to breathe in. Always make sure you've got a flat, stable substrate. If your floor isn't level, your large-format stone tiles will telegraph those imperfections, or worse, crack. Finally, don't rush to use the area before the sealer has fully cured. That sealer needs time to do its job. Working with an experienced professional like us can help you sidestep these issues, ensuring your natural stone project looks fantastic and lasts for ages.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
}